Madam President  honorable members have listened very  careful late to this debate and I do  understand your concerns as you know  this is an old dispute between the EU  and the u.s. Airbus Boeing it's been  going on for 15 years there's a huge  room of papers on only this case in  Geneva and it has certainly paid the  wages of many lawyers WTO has found that  both the EU and the US have been guilty  in giving unauthorized subsidies to  Boeing and Airbus this gives the formal  right to us now and to us a little bit  later next year to impose tariffs we are  preparing and we have made that very  clear to the u.s. that we are preparing  such a list as well to have if it would  be needed however as many of you have  said we already have too many tariffs in  this world and that is why we have been  pushing for a negotiated solution we  have provided quite detailed elements of  what we could do on both sides for  existing past and future subsidies in  this area because some subsidies have  been allowed some have been condemned  some have disappeared some are in the  process of being disappeared but it has  of course to be a balanced process where  both boeing and airbus dismantle z' and  this is a process that we have asked the  the americans to engage in i have raised  it personally we are ambassador light i  sir several times I know that member  states some of you and others had passed  the same message so we've been perfectly  United there we have also proposed that  apart from disciplining ourselves that  we should set global standards because  the big problem in the future will be  other big economies who are already  today massively subsidizing their civil  aircraft and if we could put some global  standards here it would be useful  unfortunately us have not at this point  been willing to engage we will keep on  pushing of course but so far that has  not been possible and  we are here today stuck with tariffs and  of course this probe disproportionately  and that is totally deliberate from the  US side affecting the agricultural  sector I am aware and so is Phil Hogan  and the whole Commission of the social  and economic difficulties in sectors in  regions and whole countries that this is  causing this is serious and it is urgent  and that is why since quite some time we  are assessing the situation as I said in  my introduction there are several tools  at our disposal some have already been  announced a flexibility and promotion  there might be others as well but we  need to be totally clear on what we do  what are the consequences of the US  tariffs and what are consequences of  other market distortions or other  problems because we need to be able to  design target that measures effective  measures and they need to be WTO  compliant and that is what is is doing  right now I would like to reject firmly  the notion that the trade policy has  been a disadvantage for the agriculture  market on the contrary and there are  several independent reports proving that  the latest trade deals that we have done  with Japan with Canada now with  Singapore entering into force and also  with future agreements with Vietnam  Mercosur Mexico are and will be hugely  beneficial to the European agriculture  sector we're opening up new markets for  products that are highly in demand from  all over the world and this is of course  also very important for Agriculture's  having said that I do not in any way  want to dismiss the problems that  farmers and the agriculture and the wine  sector are facing here today and this is  why our services in this commission and  the coming Commission will finalize the  study and the analysis and come back to  you and Member States we discussed it at  the trade council meeting last week with  member states as well very soon on  further action in this field thank you  very much madam president for this  debate feeling for commerce aaron
 

